"Proceedings of the International Congress of Mathematicians 2026 - Volume 5: Invited Lectures (Sections 9–11) | Wild Dynamics on Manifolds (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/25M1810787)\nciteturn10view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/25M1810787\",\"lineno\":324}); Total lines: 1151\nL291: Using a blender, Bonatti and Díaz construct a wild hyperbolic set such that a dense subset of its \\(C^1\\)-perturbations is formed by dynamics displaying periodic points whose eigenvalues are all equal to 1. Such periodic points can be \\(C^1\\)-perturbed to lie into a periodic spot.\nL292: Then we achieve the proof by invoking the Ruelle–Takens theorem [cite103†RT71 ], which implies that any direct embedding \\(\\mathbb B\\hookrightarrow \\mathbb R^n\\) can be \\(C^1\\)-approximated by a primitive renormalization of a perturbation of the identity.  □\nL293: Sketch of proof of Theorem 2.7.\nL294: \nL295: We first perturb the elliptic point to make it integrable and nondegenerate. Then it is accumulated by “integrable” parabolic periodic points. Consequently there are primitive renormalizations of neighborhoods of them in which the symplectomorphism is a linear parabolic map. This can be easily perturbed to a rational rotation. This gives the density of symplectomorphisms displaying periodic spots.\nL296: We can perturb these spots to the time-\\(\\epsilon\\) of a product of pendulum flows. Such displays a flat homoclinic tangency. Then, in dimension 2, we can apply Theorem cite95†2.2 , which produces renormalizations approximating any generalized Hénon maps. Finally, it suffices to recall that any symplectic map can be approximated by a primitive renormalization of a generalized Hénon map [cite91†Tu03 , cite104†BT25a ].  □\nL297: \nL298: Sketch of proof of Theorem 2.8.\nL299: Using a wild hyperbolic horseshoe, with both area contracting and expanding periodic points, Turaev showed the existence of a locally dense set of perturbations exhibiting periodic spots. Then he strengthened the Ruelle–Takens theorem by proving that any direct \\(C^\\infty\\)-embedding \\(G\\) of \\(\\mathbb B^n\\) into \\(\\mathbb R^n\\) can be approximated by a primitive renormalization of a \\(C^\\infty\\)-perturbation \\(g\\) of the identity.  □\nL300: With Helfter and Gourmelon, we proved the following improvement of the second part of the latter argument (the approximation becomes an equality).\nL301: \nL302: Theorem 2.10 ([cite105†BGH24 ]).\nL303: \nL304: For any \\(1\\le r \\le \\infty\\) and any orientation preserving \\(G\\in \\textrm{Diff}^r(\\mathbb B^n)\\), in any neighborhood \\(N\\subset \\textrm{Diff}^r_c (\\mathbb B^n)\\) of the identity, there exists \\(g\\in N\\) such that a primitive renormalization of \\(g\\) is equal to \\(G\\).\nL305: \nL306: Question 2.11.\nL307: Given any \\(G\\in \\textrm{Symp}^\\infty (\\mathbb B^n)\\) and any neighborhood \\(N\\subset \\textrm{Symp}^\\infty (\\mathbb B^n)\\) of the identity, is there \\(g\\in N\\) such that a primitive renormalization of \\(g\\) is equal to \\(G?\\)\nL308: \nL309: We remark that the following problem remains open.\nL310: \nL311: Problem 2.12.\nL312: \nL313: Show that for any manifold \\(M\\) of dimension \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(2\\le r\\le \\infty\\), there exists a locally generic set of \\(\\textrm{Diff}^r(M)\\) formed by \\(C^r\\)-universal maps.\nL314: To solve this problem, in view of [cite91†Tu03 , cite105†BGH24 ], it suffices to answer the following.\nL315: \nL316: Problem 2.13.\nL317: \nL318: Show that for any manifold \\(M\\) of dimension \\(n\\ge 3\\), there exists a nonempty locally dense subset of \\(\\textrm{Diff}^\\infty (M)\\) formed by diffeomorphisms displaying a periodic spot.\nL319: In [cite49†BFP23 ], we showed the existence of steady Euler flows of \\(\\mathbb R^3\\) exhibiting a wild hyperbolic set. Then, given a generic such flow, the set of its Poincaré return maps form a dense subset of \\(\\textrm{Symp}^\\infty (\\mathbb D\\hookrightarrow \\mathbb R^2)\\). Also Turaev communicated to me the following.\nL320: \nL321: Question 2.14.\nL322: \nL323: Does universality exist in the category of analytic surface diffeomorphisms? How about the category of conservative polynomial automorphisms?\nL324: ### 2.3 Density of positive metric entropy.\nL325: \nL326: Herman’s positive entropy conjecture [cite106†He98 ] asserted the existence of a \\(\\textrm{Symp}^\\infty\\)-perturbation \\(f\\) of the identity on the disk exhibiting positive metric entropy \\(h_{\\textrm{Leb}}\\, (f)\\):\nL327: \nL328: \\begin{align*} h_{\\textrm{Leb}}\\, (f):= \\lim_{n\\to \\infty } \\int \\frac 1n \\log \\|Df^n\\| \\, d\\textrm{Leb}\\, \\;.\\end{align*}\nL329: \nL330: We established the following stronger result.\nL331: \nL332: Theorem 2.15 ([cite107†BT19 ]).\nL333: If \\(f\\) is a \\(C^\\infty\\)-symplectomorphism displaying an elliptic point, then it can be \\(C^\\infty\\)-perturbed to one with positive metric entropy.\nL334: \nL335: Sketch of proof.\nL336: By Theorem cite108†2.7 , the renormalizations of a perturbation of an elliptic point approximate any dynamics. We chose one which displays a stochastic sea, as depicted in Figure cite109†4 , given by a surgery (blowup of 4 fixed points) of a linear hyperbolic map of the torus. Yet the approximation may not have positive metric entropy, but it is still the case if the heteroclinic connection persists (coincidence of half stable and unstable manifolds).\nL337: To obtain such, we show that the renormalization can be equal to this approximation composed with any vertical shear \\((x,y)\\mapsto (x,y+g(x))\\). The function \\(g\\) is then found by studying the Melnikov operator in neat coordinates.  □\nL338: cite110†Image Fig 4: A stochastic sea given by the surgery of a linear hyperbolic map.\nL339: \nL340: The first step of the latter argument is constructed in [cite111†Ber23 ] in the analytic category. The following remain open.\nL341: \nL342: Problem 2.16.\nL343: \nL344: Show the existence of an analytic perturbation of the identity on the disk with positive metric entropy.\nL345: \nL346: Problem 2.17.\nL347: \nL348: Show that a perturbation of an ellipse defines a billiard with positive metric entropy.\nL349: \nL350: Problem 2.18.\nL351: Show that an integrable surface geodesic flow can be perturbed to display a positive metric entropy.\nL352: \nL353: The proof Theorem cite112†2.15 might be useful to solve these problems. However, the heteroclinic connections appearing in this argument are very fragile; also they cannot appear among entire symplectomorphisms [cite113†Us80 ], such as the conservative Hénon map or the Chirikov standard map.\nL354: For any symplectic surface \\((M,\\Omega )\\), either (a) zero metric entropy is a locally generic property in \\(\\textrm{Symp}^\\infty (M)\\) or (b) positive metric entropy is an open-dense property in \\(\\textrm{Symp}^\\infty (M)\\).\nL355: \nL356: Question 2.19 (compare with [cite106†He98 , Q. 4.3]).\nL357: \nL358: Is (a) or (b) true?\nL359: By Theorem cite112†2.15 , to show the density of positive metric entropy, it suffices to show that if a surface symplectomorphism is weak-\\(*\\) stable (none of its perturbations displays an elliptic point), then it is uniformly hyperbolic: a symplectic counterpart of a conjecture by Mañe.\nL360: ### 2.4 Generic local property and Kolmogorov typicality.\nL361: \nL362: Let \\(\\mathcal F(M)\\) be a Fréchet manifold formed by dynamics such as \\(\\textrm{Diff}^r(M)\\), \\(\\textrm{Symp}^r(M)\\), for \\(1\\le r\\le \\infty\\), or open subsets of these spaces. We would like to study what properties are typical in the following sense.\nL363: \nL364: Definition 2.20.\nL365: A property \\(( P)\\) is Kolmogorov typical in \\(\\mathcal F(M)\\) if it is \\(d\\)-Kolmogorov typical for every \\(d\\ge 0\\) (see Definition cite114†1.5 ). The property \\((P)\\) is locally Kolmogorov typical if there exists a nonempty open subset \\({\\cal V}\\subset \\mathcal F(M)\\) in which \\((P)\\) is Kolmogorov typical.\nL366: \nL367: Note that any property which is Kolmogorov typical is topologically generic (take \\(d=0\\)). In order to study the converse, we introduced in [cite115†Ber19b ] the following notion.\nL368: \nL369: Definition 2.21.\nL370: A property \\((P)\\) is localizable if, for any nonempty open set \\(U\\subset M\\), there exists a map close to the identity, compactly supported \\(g\\in \\textrm{Symp}^\\infty (U)\\) such that \\((P)\\) holds for any \\(f\\in \\textrm{Symp}^\\infty (M)\\) satisfying \\(f^N|_U=g\\) for some \\(N\\geq 1\\). It is openly localizable if \\(g\\) can be chosen so that the property \\((P)\\) is satisfied by any map in a neighborhood in \\(\\textrm{Symp}^\\infty (M)\\) of the set of maps \\(f\\) with an iterate coinciding with \\(g\\).\nL371: For instance, exhibiting a wild hyperbolic set is an openly localizable property in any \\(\\textrm{Diff}(M)\\) for manifolds \\(M\\) of dimension \\(\\ge 2\\).\nL372: \nL373: Definition 2.22.\nL374: \nL375: A property \\((P)\\) is generically localizable if it is implied by the conjunction of a countable family of openly localizable properties \\(\\{(P_i): {i\\ge 0}\\}\\):\nL376: \nL377: \\begin{align*}\\bigwedge_i (P_i)\\Rightarrow (P)\\;.\\end{align*}\nL378: For instance, by Theorems cite108†2.7 and cite116†2.10 , universality is a generically localizable property in \\(\\textrm{Symp}^\\infty (M)\\) and in \\(\\textrm{Diff}^r(M)\\) for every \\(\\infty \\ge r\\ge 2\\). Note also that the superexponential growth of the number of periodic points is a generically localizable property (see [cite115†Ber19b , Prop. 2.2]).\nL379: \nL380: We can now state the following.\nL381: \nL382: Conjecture 2.23 ([cite115†Ber19b ]).\nL383: There are many spaces of dynamical systems for which any generically localizable property is locally Kolmogorov typical.\nL384: \nL385: This conjecture has been proved for the space of symplectomorphisms.\nL386: \nL387: Theorem 2.24 ([cite100†BT25b ]).\nL388: For every symplectic manifold \\((M,\\Omega )\\), any generic localizable property is Kolmogorov typical in the open set \\(\\textrm{Symp}^\\infty_{e\\ell } (M)\\) of symplectomorphisms possessing a nondegenerate totally elliptic point. In particular, universality is Kolmogorov typical in \\(\\textrm{Symp}^\\infty_{e\\ell } (M)\\). The same occurs with the superexponential growth of the number of periodic points.\nL389: The Kolmogorov typicality of the superexponential growth of the number of periodic points was proved for surface symplectomorphisms by Asaoka [cite117†Asa17 ].\nL390: ## 3 Emergence.\nL391: \nL392: When a dynamical system on a compact manifold \\(M\\) preserves the Lebesgue measure, Birkhoff’s ergodic theorem implies that the following sequence converges for Lebesgue almost every \\(x \\in M\\)\nL393: \nL394: \\begin{align*} {\\mathsf e}_n(x) := \\frac 1n \\sum_{0\\le k\\lt n }\\delta_{f^k(x)} \\end{align*}\nL395: to a probability measure \\({\\mathsf e}(x)\\) called the empirical measure of \\(x\\). The empirical measure describes the statistical behavior of the orbit of \\(x\\). The distribution of these statistics \\(\\{{\\mathsf e}(x)\\, :\\, x\\in M\\}\\) is given by the pushforward \\({\\mathsf e}_\\star \\textrm{Leb}\\,\\) of the Lebesgue measure onto the space of probability measures \\({\\cal M}(M)\\). The measure \\({\\mathsf e}_\\star \\textrm{Leb}\\,\\) is a measure on the space of measures. It is called the ergodic decomposition.\nL396: When the Lebesgue measure is ergodic, then \\({\\mathsf e}(x)=\\textrm{Leb}\\,\\) a.e. Hence the ergodic decomposition is a Dirac measure at \\(\\textrm{Leb}\\,\\). If the dynamics is almost finitely ergodic, then there exists a finite set of probability measures \\(\\{\\mu_1, \\dots, \\mu_k\\}\\) to which \\({\\mathsf e}(x)\\) belongs for almost every \\(x\\in M\\). Then the ergodic decomposition is a finite sum of atoms \\(\\sum_i \\lambda_i \\delta_{\\mu_i}\\).\nL397: Note that the identity is not finitely ergodic: its ergodic decomposition is \\(\\int_M \\delta_x \\, d\\textrm{Leb}\\,\\). However, it is “finite-dimensional.”\nL398: In contrast to Boltzmann’s ergodic hypothesis, we may ask when the ergodic decomposition is far from finite-dimensional. To quantify this, we introduced the notion of emergence [cite59†Ber17 ]. Let us recall its definition. We endow the space of probability measures with the Kantorovich–Wasserstein distance:\nL399: \nL400: \\begin{align*} d: (\\mu_1, \\mu_2)\\in {\\cal M}(M)\\mapsto \\sup_{\\phi \\in \\textrm{Lip}^1(M)} \\int \\phi \\, d(\\mu_1-\\mu_2)\\;. \\end{align*}\nL401: We recall that this distance endows \\({\\cal M}(M)\\) with the weak-\\(\\star\\)-topology.\nL402: \nL403: Definition 3.1.\nL404: \nL405: The emergence \\({\\cal E}(\\epsilon )\\) at scale \\(\\epsilon \\gt 0\\) is the minimal number \\(N\\) of probability measures \\((\\mu_i)_{1\\le i \\le N}\\) such that\nL406: \nL407: \\begin{align*} \\int_{x\\in M} \\min_{1\\le i \\le N} d({\\mathsf e}(x), \\mu_i) \\, d\\textrm{Leb}\\, \\lt \\epsilon \\;.\\end{align*}\nL408: \nL409: We say that the emergence is high if \\({\\cal E}(\\epsilon )\\) is not dominated by a power of \\(\\frac 1\\epsilon\\):\nL410: \\begin{align} \\limsup_{\\epsilon \\to 0} \\frac { \\log {\\cal E}(\\epsilon ) } {|\\log \\epsilon |} =\\infty.\\end{align}\nL411: \nL412: (High Emergence)\nL413: \n"
